# Model-source upstream revision

What implementing a reviewed model unit proves wrong in that design or its inherited settings and space interfaces, and what is repaired there. Every selected model-source export answers for the model unit it realizes.

## Design revision from model-source work {#design-revision-from-model-source-work}

Implementation tests whether model design fixes constructible parts, extents, pivots, writable interfaces, parameter domains, contacts, topology, open and closed boundaries, negative spaces, attribute preservation, stable ranges, bounds, and derived-data behavior. Report any geometry operation, joint, morph, attachment, normal, UV, skin, range, bound, or invalidation rule the export could not implement without choosing locally. Repair the model or earliest upstream owner before changing source; helper convenience, imported asset defaults, and one successful example do not authorize design.

`@evidence` names every upstream target repaired and the implementation case that exposed it. When the export exposes no defect, `@evidenceExclude` names its model parent and the concrete parts, dimensions, boundaries, attributes, interfaces, domains, and derived data implemented as written. A parentless export or generic fidelity statement covers nothing.

Review question: what did implementing and measuring this model-source export prove wrong in its actual model or earlier parents, or which concrete geometry-result and interface decisions did it test and find sufficient?

Sources: [glTF 2.0 on meshes, nodes, skins, morph targets, and animations](https://registry.khronos.org/glTF/specs/2.0/glTF-2.0.html); [NASA on design-to-implementation traceability](https://www.nasa.gov/reference/systems-engineering-handbook/)
